{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And you shall sharpen them.</b> It is a term of sharpening — that they should be sharp in your mouth, so that if a person asks you a thing you would not need to stammer over it, but answer him at once (Sifrei; Kiddushin 30a)." "<b>To your children.</b> These are the disciples — we find in every place that disciples are called children, as it says ״בָּנִים אַתֶּם לַה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״ (Deuteronomy 14), and it says ״בְּנֵי הַנְּבִיאִים אֲשֶׁר בֵּית אֵל״ (II Kings 2), and so with Hezekiah, who taught Torah to all Israel and called them children, as it says ״בָּנַי עַתָּה אַל תִּשָּׁלוּ״ (II Chronicles 29). And just as disciples are called children (as it says ״בָּנִים אַתֶּם לַה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״), so the teacher is called father, as it says ״אָבִי אָבִי רֶכֶב יִשְׂרָאֵל וְגוֹ׳״ (II Kings 2) (Sifrei)." "<b>And you shall speak of them.</b> That the essence of your speech should be only in them — make them primary and do not make them secondary (ibid.)." "<b>And when you lie down.</b> One might think even if he lay down at midday — Scripture says <b>and when you rise up</b>. One might think even if he rose at midnight — Scripture says <b>when you sit in your house and when you walk on the way</b> — the Torah spoke in the way of the world: the time of lying down and the time of rising (ibid.)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:46:13.541758407Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}